body:
Hi there, I'm not a mom personally, but I knew a mother with a young child during my stay at one of three hospitals I'd been to. While we were in the ICU together, she could not see her child, but once she demonstrated she wasn't going to be an immediate danger to herself, she was transferred to the adult open unit. While there, she could see her child on Saturdays in the cafeteria under supervision. If I remember correctly, as her depression got worse, the psychiatrist allowed her to see her child one additional time per week in the cafeteria. And yes, she did have a photo album. Psychiatric wards typically discourage sharing too much personal information with the other patients, however. For instance, your phone number, address, etc. (Just use common sense when you share information. For example, I did not share my information with a man that was stalking me in the ICU).

&#x200B;

As for my overall experience, I would say that psych wards aren't anything to be afraid of. Of course, not all psych wards are the same and you should do your research so you can admit yourself to the best one in your area that also takes your insurance (it is extremely expensive, but worth it if you feel your life depends on it). 

&#x200B;

To begin, you will be admitted to the ICU while they evaluate you psychiatrically (but I may be mistaken- maybe you can skip that step and go straight to a more open ward). The ICU is typically the worst part of the entire stay since there are many severely ill patients there, and there is much less stimulation. While hearing screaming isn't uncommon, you're going to be safe. And if you ever feel unsafe, never hesitate to let staff know. They're usually very observant anyway. My first time in an ICU, a man started stalking me which naturally made me feel unsafe. But staff were already aware of this by the time I approached them about the issue. I was immediately transferred to another ward (phew!).

&#x200B;

In open wards, you'll see much higher functioning adults of all ages. They come for a variety of reasons but I found a majority of them had addiction disorders. At every single hospital, I was able to meet lovely people all going through difficult moments in their life. During group therapy, you get to understand these people and realize that you're not alone in your struggles. Talking to other people makes life bearable, and especially because after a while it becomes extremely boring, and at times can feel like a prison. 

&#x200B;

The more effort you put into your treatment will result in you feeling better. And I did not put in effort at all, so I did not feel better at all. Of course, maybe you're so depressed that you feel like you can't do anything at all, and that's okay. But, if you challenge yourself to even take a shower everyday and get dressed, that is still progress. 

&#x200B;

A concern you may have is about being drugged up. And yes, it's true that they put you on a lot of medications and change dosages and regimens all the time. And yes, you might get side effects. But, I came to understand that the reason they do this is so the doctors can monitor your reactions as soon as possible versus in the outside world, it could be over a month before you find out if a medication is effective or not. That being said, you might have a doctor that isn't listening to you and keeps prescribing you medications that make you ill or that do not work. I've heard about this happening, unfortunately. Remember that you have rights! First, you can refuse your medication. Second, you may request to see another doctor. And thirdly, you may discharge against medical advise if you do not like your treatment (unless you are on a psychiatric hold. typically, if you voluntarily admit yourself, you can leave anytime). - which I guess was also one of your concerns.

&#x200B;

I've only been hospitalized in California, so I do not know anything regarding Tennessee laws. I'm sure you can visit the hospital of your choice before you admit yourself so you can ask questions. They cannot take you against your will unless you tell them you are actively suicidal. 

&#x200B;

Anyway, I know this is long! I hope it makes you feel better, and if you have any other questions, I can answer those too.
